### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

<html>
<body>
<!--StartFragment--><html><head></head><body><h1><span>Proposal: Introduce a native browser navigation API supporting arbitrary HTTP methods</span></h1><h2><span>Summary</span></h2><p><span>I would like to propose introducing a new native JavaScript API, tentatively named </span><code><span>window.location.request()</span></code><span>, that allows developers to initiate a browser navigation using arbitrary HTTP methods and request data, while preserving the normal browser navigation experience.</span></p><p><span>The main objective is to combine two capabilities that currently exist separately:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>The flexibility of HTTP requests, including different methods and request bodies.</span></p></li><li><p><span>The native browser navigation behavior, where the server response is loaded and displayed directly in a browsing context.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Currently, developers can achieve either one or the other, but there is no straightforward native API that combines both.</span></p><h2><span>1. The problem</span></h2><p><span>JavaScript currently provides several ways to interact with remote resources.</span></p><p><span>For example:</span></p><pre><code><span>window.location.href = '/page.php';</span></code></pre><p><span>This performs a GET navigation.</span></p><p><span>The Fetch API provides much more flexibility:</span></p><pre><code><span>fetch('/api/resource', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify({ id: 123 }),
    headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json'
    }
});</span></code></pre><p><span>However, Fetch does not perform a browser navigation. It returns a response to JavaScript, and developers must manually process that response.</span></p><p><span>HTML forms provide native navigation with POST:</span></p><pre><code><span>&lt;form action="/page.php" method="POST"&gt;
    &lt;input type="hidden" name="id" value="123"&gt;
    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Continue&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</span></code></pre><p><span>However, native HTML form submissions have limited HTTP method support.</span></p><p><span>There is currently no simple native mechanism that allows developers to navigate to a resource using methods such as PUT, PATCH, DELETE, OPTIONS or custom HTTP methods, while displaying the resulting response as a normal browser navigation.</span></p><h2><span>2. Current workaround</span></h2><p><span>To perform a POST navigation dynamically, developers often have to create an HTML form through JavaScript:</span></p><pre><code><span>function post(url, params, target = '_self') {
    const form = document.createElement('form');

    form.method = 'POST';
    form.action = url;
    form.target = target;

    for (const key in params) {
        const input = document.createElement('input');

        input.type = 'hidden';
        input.name = key;
        input.value = params[key];

        form.appendChild(input);
    }

    document.body.appendChild(form);
    form.submit();
    form.remove();
}</span></code></pre><p><span>This approach works, but it has several limitations:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Developers must manually create and manage DOM elements.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Arrays and complex data structures require additional handling.</span></p></li><li><p><span>JSON requests cannot be submitted directly through standard form submission.</span></p></li><li><p><span>File handling requires additional logic.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Native form submission does not provide arbitrary HTTP method support.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Developers need custom helper functions for functionality that could potentially be provided by the browser itself.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>A native API could eliminate much of this boilerplate.</span></p><h2><span>3. Proposed API</span></h2><p><span>A possible API design would be:</span></p><pre><code><span>window.location.request(
    url,
    params = {},
    method = 'GET',
    target = '_self',
    enctype
);</span></code></pre><p><span>The parameters would be:</span></p>
Parameter | Description | Default
-- | -- | --
url | Destination URL | Required
params | Request parameters or data | {}
method | HTTP method | GET
target | Destination browsing context | _self
enctype | Request body encoding | Automatically selected

<p><span>The API should return </span><code><span>undefined</span></code><span>, because its purpose is to initiate a browser navigation rather than expose an HTTP response to JavaScript.</span></p><h2><span>4. Examples</span></h2><h3><span>GET navigation</span></h3><pre><code><span>window.location.request('/search.php', {
    query: 'javascript',
    page: 1
});</span></code></pre><h3><span>POST navigation</span></h3><pre><code><span>window.location.request(
    '/documents/print.php',
    {
        id: 123,
        format: 'PDF'
    },
    'POST'
);</span></code></pre><h3><span>PUT with JSON</span></h3><pre><code><span>window.location.request(
    '/api/users/123',
    {
        name: 'John',
        active: true
    },
    'PUT',
    '_self',
    'application/json'
);</span></code></pre><h3><span>DELETE navigation</span></h3><pre><code><span>window.location.request(
    '/api/users/123',
    {},
    'DELETE'
);</span></code></pre><h3><span>POST in a new tab</span></h3><pre><code><span>window.location.request(
    '/documents/print.php',
    { id: 123 },
    'POST',
    '_blank'
);</span></code></pre><h3><span>Multipart form submission</span></h3><pre><code><span>const data = new FormData();

data.append('document', file);
data.append('description', 'Medical document');

window.location.request(
    '/documents/upload.php',
    data,
    'POST'
);</span></code></pre><p><span>These examples illustrate the intended behavior, not a finalized normative specification.</span></p><h2><span>5. HTTP method support</span></h2><p><span>The proposed API should not be restricted to GET and POST.</span></p><p><span>It should support all valid HTTP method tokens, including:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>GET</span></p></li><li><p><span>HEAD</span></p></li><li><p><span>POST</span></p></li><li><p><span>PUT</span></p></li><li><p><span>PATCH</span></p></li><li><p><span>DELETE</span></p></li><li><p><span>OPTIONS</span></p></li><li><p><span>Other registered or custom HTTP methods.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>Methods forbidden by the browser's security model would remain unavailable.</span></p><p><span>This would make the API extensible and avoid limiting it to the methods commonly used today.</span></p><h2><span>6. Request data and content types</span></h2><p><span>The API should support common JavaScript data structures:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Plain JavaScript objects.</span></p></li><li><p><span>FormData.</span></p></li><li><p><span>URLSearchParams.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>The following content types should be supported:</span></p><ul><li><p><code><span>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</span></code></p></li><li><p><code><span>multipart/form-data</span></code></p></li><li><p><code><span>text/plain</span></code></p></li><li><p><code><span>application/json</span></code></p></li></ul><p><span>The browser should handle serialization and encoding automatically.</span></p><p><span>For example, JavaScript arrays should be preserved as arrays in JSON, while form-based formats could represent them using repeated parameter names.</span></p><p><span>File and Blob objects should be supported through FormData and multipart encoding.</span></p><h2><span>7. Navigation behavior</span></h2><p><span>The key characteristic of this API is that it must perform a real browser navigation.</span></p><p><span>The server response should be loaded directly into the destination browsing context.</span></p><p><span>The proposed behavior includes:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Support for </span><code><span>_self</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>_blank</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>_parent</span></code><span>, </span><code><span>_top</span></code><span> and named browsing contexts.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Normal browser history behavior.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Normal HTTP redirect handling.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Normal document loading and rendering.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Native browser handling of HTTP error responses.</span></p></li><li><p><span>No requirement for JavaScript to manually process the returned response.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>The intention is not to replace Fetch, XMLHttpRequest or existing navigation APIs.</span></p><p><span>It is to provide a different operation: an HTTP request whose result becomes the active document of a browsing context.</span></p><h2><span>8. Security considerations</span></h2><p><span>The new API should not bypass existing browser security mechanisms.</span></p><p><span>It should remain subject to the browser's existing navigation and request security policies, including:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Content Security Policy.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Sandbox restrictions.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Popup-blocking policies.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Cookie and credential policies.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Referrer policies.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Mixed-content restrictions.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Other applicable browser security mechanisms.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>The API should use the browser's native networking and navigation infrastructure rather than implementing a separate networking mechanism in JavaScript.</span></p><h2><span>9. Why this API could be useful</span></h2><p><span>This capability could simplify several common development scenarios:</span></p><p><strong><span>Document generation</span></strong></p><p><span>Many applications generate PDFs or other documents dynamically on the server and need to submit parameters using POST while opening the result in a new tab.</span></p><p><strong><span>Traditional server-rendered applications</span></strong></p><p><span>Applications that rely on server-side rendering could navigate using different HTTP methods without implementing custom JavaScript response handling.</span></p><p><strong><span>REST-oriented applications</span></strong></p><p><span>Developers could navigate directly to the server response after PUT, PATCH or DELETE operations.</span></p><p><strong><span>File uploads and downloads</span></strong></p><p><span>A native navigation mechanism supporting multipart requests could simplify workflows involving uploaded files and server-generated responses.</span></p><p><strong><span>Custom HTTP protocols and methods</span></strong></p><p><span>Supporting valid extension methods would make the API more flexible for applications using custom server-side workflows.</span></p><h2><span>10. Questions for discussion</span></h2><p><span>I understand that this proposal would require changes to the existing HTML navigation and form submission algorithms.</span></p><p><span>In particular, I would appreciate feedback on:</span></p><ol start="1"><li><p><span>Whether a navigation API supporting arbitrary HTTP methods would be a useful addition to the HTML Standard.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Whether </span><code><span>window.location.request()</span></code><span> would be an appropriate API design or whether a different interface would fit better.</span></p></li><li><p><span>How this functionality could integrate with the existing HTML navigation model.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Which security restrictions would need to apply.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Whether there are existing technical limitations that would make this proposal impractical.</span></p></li></ol><h2><span>Conclusion</span></h2><p><span>The goal is simple:</span></p><p><span>Developers should be able to initiate a native browser navigation using an arbitrary HTTP method and request data, without manually creating HTML forms or implementing custom response handling.</span></p><p><span>The browser should take care of the request, navigation, redirects, history and rendering.</span></p><p><span>I believe this could be a useful addition to the web platform, especially for applications that rely heavily on server-side processing and document generation.</span></p></body></html><!--EndFragment-->
</body>
